+// Writer admission control driven by device read latency (opt-in; issue
+// #43, doc/design/writer-admission-control.md).
+//
+// When enabled, a write of a document above 64 KiB is delayed before it
+// takes any lock whenever the volume device's reads are queueing: the delay
+// is gain x (smoothed read await - read_latency_target), clamped to
+// [0, max_delay].  The read await comes from the kernel's per-device
+// counters (Linux, /sys/dev/block/MAJ:MIN/stat of the volume file's
+// device), sampled by writers at most every 50 ms; readers are not
+// involved.  Where the counters are not available (other platforms, tmpfs,
+// overlay) it does nothing.  Small writes are never delayed.
+//
+// Meant to be paired with fill_large_document_tail: the fill removes the
+// ext4 read-before-write that used to slow inserts on a congested device,
+// and this puts back a bounded, congestion-only brake so reads do not queue
+// behind the extra write-back.  Not persisted; C++ only (not in the C API).
+// A caller on an event loop (nginx) must keep max_delay small: the delay is
+// a sleep on the calling thread.
+struct WriteAdmissionConfig {
+  bool enabled = false;
+  // Throttling starts when the smoothed device read await exceeds this.
+  // 2 ms is the kernel's blk-wbt read target for non-rotational devices; a
+  // device that is slower than that when idle (a rotational disk, a network
+  // block device) needs a higher target.
+  std::chrono::microseconds read_latency_target{2000};
+  // Delay per unit of excess await (2.0: 1 ms over target -> 2 ms delay).
+  double gain = 2.0;
+  // Upper bound on the delay of one write.
+  std::chrono::microseconds max_delay{10000};
+};
